                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "3da7d51a-d663-41ac-ba5b-87709d5bbb77",
                        "entry_id": "1a35669e-8673-308a-b1f6-320bc07bc38d",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "birefringences"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b9b4150f-f924-3693-9848-dc0f77042dc4",
                        "entry_id": "1a35669e-8673-308a-b1f6-320bc07bc38d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The splitting of a ray of light into two parallel rays of perpendicular polarization by passage through an optically anisotropic medium; the property of a material that light passing through it is so split."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e9272eb8-dfc3-4df2-87fc-081e70fec1f3",
                                "sense_id": "b9b4150f-f924-3693-9848-dc0f77042dc4",
                                "text": "In figure 6, the birefringence, δ, for 2H, 4H, 6H, and 15R is plotted as a function of wavelength."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bb64cad4-a67c-4a68-befa-b70f63997911",
                                "sense_id": "b9b4150f-f924-3693-9848-dc0f77042dc4",
                                "text": "This means that, in contrast to the relaxation after steady shear flow, one has actually to expect that there will be a significant difference in the speeds of relaxation of birefringence and recoverable shear like the difference suggested by the courses of curves I and II of Fig.4.1."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e05aa466-e970-40c7-b90a-bed9a688c72a",
                                "sense_id": "b9b4150f-f924-3693-9848-dc0f77042dc4",
                                "text": "In this paper, two-dimensional thermal and mechanical stress-induced birefringence measurements in laser-diode-pumped solid-state laser materials using the new polarimeter are described."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dabe8d29-b1c6-4469-9c80-f2a1c444a1b0",
                                "sense_id": "b9b4150f-f924-3693-9848-dc0f77042dc4",
                                "text": "2012, Luís A. Fernandes, Birefringence and Bragg grating control in femtosecond laser written optical circuits, Departamento de Física e Astronomia da Universidade do Porto, page 112,\nHeat accumulation in borosilicate glass [23] has [been] shown to produce lower birefringence due to radial stress regions as opposed to the more asymmetric stress produced in fused silica [136, 137]; however, these features remain unexplored in most glasses."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
